course: dictionary
lesson: 5399
title: Context clues: the kookaburra
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words name things you may never have seen. Context helps you find out what they are. «The laughing kookaburra is a big kingfisher whose family groups laugh together at dawn and dusk.» The word laughing and the phrase laugh together are clues that the bird is named for its sound.
explain: Read the clues again: it is big, it is a kingfisher, and it eats snakes, lizards, insects and mice. A kookaburra is a big Australian bird of the kingfisher family with a loud call that sounds like a person laughing. Check your picture: does it fit every clue?
explain: The same word can appear in different places. «The Australian kookaburra's call sounds like loud laughter» tells you where it lives. A song uses it too: «Kookaburra sits in the old gum tree» is a famous round that children sing.
fact: A kookaburra is a big Australian bird of the kingfisher family.
fact: Kookaburras have a loud call that sounds like a person laughing.
fact: Kookaburra families laugh together at dawn and dusk. This marks their territory, the area they claim as their own.
fact: Kookaburras eat snakes, lizards, insects and mice.
fact: The plural of kookaburra is kookaburras.
exercise: «The laughing kookaburra is a big kingfisher whose family groups laugh together at dawn and dusk.» What is a kookaburra? | A small grey mouse that lives in gardens | A tree that grows in the desert | *A big Australian bird with a loud, laughing call | A fish that swims in the sea
exercise: Lee reads «The Australian kookaburra's call sounds like loud laughter.» What does call mean here? | A shout for help only | A telephone message | *The loud sound a bird makes | A song sung by a choir
exercise: Which of these does a kookaburra eat? | Gum leaves | *Lizards | Sand | Clouds
test: Why do kookaburra families laugh together at dawn and dusk? | *To mark their territory, the area they claim | To sing the round in a choir | To wake up the sea | To say goodbye to the sun
test: In the old children's round, where does the kookaburra sit? | In the sea | In a kitchen | On a rock | *In the old gum tree
exam: Based on «a kookaburra laughs from a branch», what is a kookaburra doing when it laughs? | Tossing a grape into the air | *Sitting on a branch and laughing | Swimming in a pond | Hovering over a flower
